A1. The big cheese. ~ MANAGER
A6. The head of the household (obsolete). ~ HUSBAND
A12. If you live in an apartment your meals always come from one. ~ CAN
A13. If it were not for this animal there would be no "Ukes." ~ CAT
A15. If you want to starve to death try to eat these with a knife (sing.). ~ PEA
A16. A lifeboat would be a death boat without one. ~ OAR
A18. A preposition. ~ ON
A19. Her manner is like this when you don't do as she wishes. ~ ICY
A20. All successful hunters have a good one. ~ AIM
A22. What actors do when they're working. ~ ACT
A24. A Quaker's pronoun. ~ YE
A25. The part of the road that a road hog wants. ~ ALL
A26. When wifey is away the house is never in this condition. ~ ORDER
A28. A Hawaiian tree. ~ KOA
A30. A little out of style now but many knights lost their lives trying to win them. ~ GARLANDS
A32. The man who thinks he runs our Government. ~ LOBBYIST
A34. An animal famous because of the abundance of its teeth. ~ ELK
A35. You become one when she gives you the first kiss. ~ DONEE
A37. "The female is more deadly than the male." Yes, even in the deer family. ~ DOE
A38. Some winds blow in this direction, but they are not necessarily ill ones (abbr.). ~ SE
A39. A girl's name. One of the old-fashioned kind. (We mean the name.) ~ AMY
A41. Born. ~ NEE
A42. The busy little insect who toils while the grasshopper sings. ~ ANT
A44. A friend in need is a friend indeed, especially when you are thirsty (abbr.). ~ DR
A45. A literary composition. ~ ESSAY
A47. To be on the lookout for her next move. ~ ALERT
A49. Posses. ~ HAS
A50. What go-getters do. ~ GET
A51. Spanish for river. Sorry but these words will creep in. ~ RIO
A52. To be somewhat ill. ~ AIL
A54. What football stars have to do to their bones after the big game. ~ RESET
A57. The sap from a pine tree. No, this has no connection with Darwin's theory. ~ RESIN
A60. No day is complete without one. ~ AM
A62. One of those fellows who has a girl in every port. ~ TAR
A63. A hallroom bedstead. ~ COT
A65. Something sprinters get before they start in a race. ~ SET
A66. A second-hand flivver can do anything but this. ~ GO
A67. What hammocks do when two heavyweights sit in them. ~ SAG
A69. For the fourth time she orders this. (If she doesn't get it pretty soon she's going to another restaurant.) ~ SALAD
A71. A summer coat. (Summer coats and summer not!) ~ TAN
A72. A globe trotter. ~ TRAVELER
A74. If Goliath and Tom Thumb were placed side by side they would make a great one. ~ CONTRAST
A76. The original wind instrument artist. ~ PAN
A77. Something the gentle sex never do when they're defeated in a battle of words. (Possibly because they never are.) ~ ADMIT
A79. Something to sit on. ~ LAP
A80. If you are a crossword fan you won't have to use a dictionary to find out what a printer's measure is. ~ EM
A82. Bobbed hair has just about run the manufacturer of this article out of business. ~ NET
A84. A member of the upper 400 is euphemistically called a "toper" instead of this. ~ SOT
A85. If you lose this don't advertise it in the papers. ~ REP
A86. An adverb meaning "in like manner." ~ SO
A87. "Abie's Irish Rose" when she did this on a tack. ~ SAT
A89. Nowadays when this prodigal has a homecoming instead of killing the fatted calf, they shoot the bull. ~ SON
A91. What an optimist calls his flivver. ~ CAR
A92. As the Romans say, "This is nothing." ~ NIL
A93. Something all of us hope we won't be in the hereafter. ~ TOASTED
A94. (Dodecatheon meadia.) If that will help you, if not this is a perennial herb, a member of the primrose family, and a famous cow pasture flower. ~ COWSLIP

D1. An exclusive product of the Golden West—Ask any Californian. ~ MAN
D2. Indefinite article. ~ AN
D3. Shouts applause. ~ ACCLAIMS
D4. A word aptly applied to most bachelors. ~ GAY
D5. What a North Carolina backwoodsman did to his dinner. ~ ET
D7. Where we all want to go to spend eternity. ~ UP
D8. Something that can always be found near the seashore. ~ SEA
D9. The part of anatomy we all sadly lack when we ask her old man for her hand. ~ BACKBONE
D10. What a few women say when they mean no. ~ NO
D11. This breaks with great regularity. ~ DAY
D12. To accumulate as a crowd in the ten-cent store before Xmas. ~ CONGEST
D14. If you go to a dance without one you are apt to be thrown out on your head. (Collegiate.) ~ BID
D17. Marry on love alone and you will be one of these for the rest of your life (pl.). ~ RENTERS
D19. How little Willie felt after eating too many green apples. ~ ILL
D20. What they had Abie indicted for when his three-ball joint went up in smoke. ~ ARSON
D21. If you and your wife have one she will probably get a divorce. ~ MELEE
D23. A plaything. ~ TOY
D25. This got safely over the first water hazard. ~ ARK
D26. What triplets are. ~ ODD
D27. This made shad an aristocratic fish. ~ ROE
D29. The first thing to render after your friend has imbibed too much wood alcohol. ~ AID
D31. If the man referred to above had been drinking this our assistance would have been unnecessary. ~ ALE
D33. That which covers our last resting place. ~ SOD
D36. Not old. ~ NEW
D39. What some authorities claim a woman's ability to lie to be. ~ ASSET
D40. Do you know there are two ways to spell "jager"? ~ YAGER
D42. Inspissated juice of the wood agallochum. (How is that for a sticker?) ~ ALOES
D43. A peculiar characteristic. ~ TRAIT
D45. When you wish to keep a secret there is one in every wall. ~ EAR
D46. "I'll get you—Georgette!" ~ YET
D47. What your girl gave you when she found a new sheik. ~ AIR
D48. A synonym for money. ~ TIN
D53. All love-sick couples build them in the air. ~ CASTLES
D55. Monotony's twin sister. ~ SAMENESS
D56. The sun. ~ SOL
D58. In the early days of our history these people traveled in covered wagons. ~ SETTLERS
D59. This is hard to maintain if you are the father of a flapper. ~ CONTROL
D61. Deface. ~ MAR
D63. "Aunty Everything" would have us do without these also. ~ CARDS
D64. Implied. ~ TACIT
D66. Care should be used in stepping on this. ~ GAS
D68. When she begins to do this it is time to say "Good night." ~ GAP
D69. If you have been real industrious No. 8, vertical, told you all about this one. ~ SEA
D70. This goes with a dash. ~ DOT
D71. Something no crook likes to feel on his shoulder. ~ TAP
D73. This is very moving. ~ VAN
D75. What Poe heard at his bedroom door. ~ RAP
D78. What you have to do to the kitchen floor after your home-brew has exploded. ~ MOP
D81. A place where you'll often find a welcome. ~ MAT
D83. A small child. ~ TOT
D85. In collegiate circles this term is applied to a young lad fresh from the country. ~ RAW
D86. Many a one of these comes from the hip. ~ SIP
D88. That little preposition you simply can't keep out. ~ TO
D90. Winds often blow in this direction too. (abbr.) ~ NE
D91. The third partner of that famous concern. Sears Robuck & Co. ~ CO
D92. The metal that has made the buffalo famous. (abbr.) ~ NI


